# bootstrap: the stand-in system the base set is first built in (docs/RECIPES.md, "The bootstrap"). It is the one recipe
# that is built against the host, and nothing of it ships: build/kits/bootstrap.sh lays the base set's own kits over it
# as they are built (stage 1), then builds the set again in a root of those kits alone (stage 2), and again (stage 3).
#
# The method is Linux From Scratch's (12.4, chapters 5 and 6, the same versions): a cross toolchain in tools/ that
# does not look at the host's libraries, the C library built with it into the new root, then the tools every build
# needs, cross-compiled so that they run on that C library and nothing else. What comes out is a merged /usr that is
# a whole small system: a compiler for C and C++, the binary tools, the C library, a shell and the usual tools.
# The host needs: gcc and g++, make, bison, gawk, m4, makeinfo, patch, xz, python3.
